(project pricklypear-forge
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: forge — executable spec graphs; borge's
       workflow as habitat data. LLMs draft and review; mechanical checks
       flag anomalies; the human gates each level of the descent.")
 (status planned)
 (convention ocaml-dune)

 (* agent note (|Design rationale, from the 2026-08 conversation that
   produced this chapter:
   - "Procedural borge": the spec stops being a document you consult and
     becomes a program you run. Phase transitions are driven by code, not
     by a human typing plan/make/review.
   - Victory conditions are the artifact; code is a cache. If codegen
     trends free, the expensive scarce thing is the VERIFIED SPEC GRAPH —
     every node carrying its win condition, provably green. Code is
     re-derivable output of the graph.
   - The frontier is computed, not judged: free-vars over drafted bodies
     derives what is missing; build order is a topological walk, not an
     LLM guess.
   - Mechanical flags first, judgment as residue: capability escalation,
     missing victory condition, dangling refs, cycles, orphans — all
     computable. The human reviews a list of anomalies, not a wall of
     nodes. Review fatigue is the quiet killer of every human-in-the-
     loop system; the defense is surfacing LESS over time, not more.
   - Human gates each level of the tree descent. Mistakes get cheaper
     as you go down; attention is allocated top-heavy because blast
     radius is top-heavy. Progressive disclosure of commitment: you are
     never asked to review more than one delta of abstraction at a time.
   - The loop lives IN the habitat (a Nopales package), not in the
     laptop harness. Separation of powers: drafter and driver must not
     be the same mind. The harness agent is demoted to drafter; the
     human answers the loop via the flag queue, does not drive it.
   - Reroll is INFORMED: reroll-with-note attaches a constraint to the
     node; accumulated constraints are a durable style guide the drafter
     cannot drift from. Otherwise reroll is a slot machine.
   - UI LAST, on evidence: you cannot design the approval UI until you
     know which flags actually fire in practice. The boring anomaly
     queue beats the gorgeous graph explorer. Build the loop headless;
     the queue teaches you what to draw.|) *)

 (section forge
  (doc "Executable spec: the borge discipline as a living graph of typed
        nodes in the habitat. A spec is a tree; each node carries a
        machine-checkable victory condition; LLM drafters fill leaves
        bottom-up along a computed frontier; mechanical checkers flag
        anomalies; the human approves each level before the descent
        continues.")
  (status planned)

  (details
   (doc (|
        =========================================================
        WHAT THIS CHAPTER IS
        =========================================================
        Borge today is a CLI over .borg FILES. This chapter is the
        same discipline re-expressed as HABITAT DATA: the spec is a
        tree of typed nodes, the workflow is a Nopales interpreter
        loop running inside the image, LLM calls are drafting and
        review passes over nodes, and the human's plan/make/review
        commands become gates the loop waits on. Nothing about the
        trust model changes: the human promotes, the human gates,
        mechanical checks run before any judgment is asked for.

        The package is libs/forge — pure Nopales, product-shaped,
        living on branches like every other product lib. It gains
        ONE new kernel prim (forge-effects, below) and reuses
        everything else: tree nodes, data rows, branching prims,
        the ai-authoring LLM surface, the jobs runner, and the
        notes/log/docs conventions of habitat-workflow.

        =========================================================
        THE GRAPH (node shapes, one-tree placement)
        =========================================================
        A spec graph is a set of typed data nodes. Per one-tree,
        code-side nodes live under the branch lens; graph STATE
        (status, review verdicts) is shared data, keyed by owning
        user, under data.shared.forge.* — same placement argument
        as jobs: the loop (host identity) must mutate node rows
        regardless of who is drafting.

          forge-node    data.shared.forge-node.<graph>.<path>
          forge-flag    data.shared.forge-flag.<node-path>
          forge-graph   data.shared.forge-graph.<name>

        forge-node fields:
          graph        string   owning graph name
          path         string   "root/auth/login" — position in tree
          kind         enum     requirement | module | function | test
          title        string   one-line purpose (human-readable)
          body         string   Nopales source when kind=function; else ""
          verify       string   Nopales source of the victory check
          effects      string   declared badge (pure|read-db|write-db|…)
          status       enum     planned | drafted | verified | flagged
                              | approved | implemented
          constraints  json     accumulated reroll-with-note strings
          parent       string   path of parent node ("" for root)
          children     json     list of child paths (denormalized index)
          branch       string   code lens this node was drafted on
          author       string   whoami at last mutation

        forge-flag fields:
          node_path, check (enum: capability|victory|dangling|cycle|
          orphan|size|judgment), detail string, resolved bool,
          resolution string ("" until a human or the loop closes it)

        forge-graph fields:
          name, root_path, status (planning|descending|verifying|done|
          stalled), created_by, created_at

        The DENORMALIZED children index exists because backlinks over
        body strings is the wrong query; children must be enumerable
        without parsing. parent is the canonical link; children is
        maintained by the same mutation fns (one transaction).

        =========================================================
        THE FRONTIER (planning is a computation, not a judgment)
        =========================================================
        The single strongest idea in this design: build order is
        DERIVED FROM CODE, not guessed by an LLM.

          free-vars:    walk a parsed body; collect symbols that are
                        not bound by any enclosing lambda/let/define
                        and are not kernel prims or existing bindings.
          unresolved:   free-vars filtered against bound names.
          frontier:     all planned nodes whose unresolved set is
                        empty — leaves whose dependencies all exist.
          stub-children: when a draft's free-vars reference names
                        that exist NOWHERE (not prim, not binding,
                        not a sibling node), those names become NEW
                        planned child nodes. The drafter invented a
                        helper; the graph records the debt.

        Bottom-up falls out: a node can only be DRAFTED when its
        dependencies are all at least drafted. The frontier is
        recomputed after every mutation. There is no planner prompt;
        there is a graph query.

        Cycles: if drafting A produces a stub B whose drafting
        references A, the cycle check flags it. Recursion is fine
        in Nopales but breaks the topological frontier — a cycle
        means the level needs restructuring (mutual-recursion
        node, or a declared base-case split), and that is a
        HUMAN decision, so the flag goes to judgment.

        =========================================================
        MECHANICAL FLAGS (checks that run before the human wakes)
        =========================================================
        Each flagger is a small pure-ish function over the graph.
        They run after every draft and after every level-descent
        proposal. The approval surface shows FLAGS, not nodes.

          capability   child's inferred effects (via forge-effects
                       prim) exceed the parent's declared badge.
                       "read-only view" containing (create ...) is
                       caught here, not by eyeballing.
          victory      node has empty verify source. Unapprovable
                       by definition; flagged before human sees it.
          dangling     free-vars reference nothing: not prim, not
                       binding, not a sibling/parent node. Either a
                       missing stub or a typo'd name; both worth a
                       flag.
          cycle        frontier math found a dependency loop.
          orphan       node traces to no requirement ancestor.
                       Gold-plating detector: completeness means
                       every requirement has a node AND every node
                       traces to a requirement; no orphans either
                       direction.
          size         drafted body exceeds a review-in-one-glance
                       threshold (default ~20 forms). Suggests a
                       split; not a hard error.
          judgment     the drafter itself asked for clarification.
                       The "flag for review/clarification" of the
                       tri-state review. This is the log/issues
                       escape hatch, node-scoped.

        The one new kernel surface:

          (forge-effects "name-or-src")  → badge string
              Runs lisp/lib/effect.ml infer over the named binding
              (or parsed source) and returns EffSet.badge. This is
              the SAME analysis install-function already gates on;
              this prim merely EXPOSES it. Read-only, Pure effect.

        =========================================================
        THE LOOP (the interpreter that replaces plan/make/review)
        =========================================================
        One function, forge/step, is the whole state machine. It is
        deliberately boring:

          (forge/step graph-name)
            1. If unresolved judgment flags exist → do nothing;
               the loop is BLOCKED on the human. Return the flag
               list. (The human's queue IS the control flow.)
            2. Else if the current level has unapproved nodes →
               run mechanical flaggers, present the level for
               approval, stop. HUMAN GATE.
            3. Else if the frontier is non-empty → pick one node,
               assemble a draft prompt (context: title, parent
               declaration, constraints, sibling summaries, the
               platform-ref note), call the drafter (agent-session
               + agent-say, or llm-seed for leaf functions), parse
               the result into body+verify, run mechanical flaggers,
               write the node. Return what changed.
            4. Else → the level is complete; propose the descent
               (stub children of approved nodes) and stop. HUMAN
               GATE on the new level.

        forge/run is forge/step in a job, so the loop persists
        across HTTP requests and survives the laptop disconnecting.
        The jobs chapter supplies the fiber; forge supplies the
        step function. One job per graph, re-submitted after each
        step while status is not done/stalled.

        TRI-STATE REVIEW: when a node's draft passes mechanical
        checks it goes to LLM review (a SECOND agent session, not
        the drafter's) which returns approve | deny | flag. Deny
        → redraft with the review note appended to constraints.
        Flag → judgment flag, human queue. Approve → status
        verified (verify source passes) or stays drafted (verify
        needs a human run). Separation of powers is structural:
        drafter, reviewer, and driver are three different sessions.

        REROLL-WITH-NOTE: the human's reject carries a reason.
        The reason appends to the node's constraints, and every
        subsequent draft prompt includes all constraints. The
        graph accumulates taste; the drafter cannot drift from a
        constraint it has been given twice.

        =========================================================
        THE HUMAN SURFACE (triage, not review)
        =========================================================
        v1 is deliberately minimal — the UI is deferred on
        PRINCIPLE (you cannot design the approval surface until
        you know which flags fire). Phase one surfaces:

          /forge              list graphs + status + open flag count
          /forge/<name>       one graph: current level's nodes with
                              flags, grouped by flag type; approve /
                              reroll(with note) / veto per node;
                              approve-all-clean button
          log/issues          judgment flags land here too, one line
                              each, per habitat-workflow

        The approval page shows ANOMALIES. A node with zero flags
        and a passing verify renders as one line: title, badge,
        approve button. A flagged node renders its flag detail
        first. The human reads flags, not code, unless they choose
        to expand. This is the review-fatigue defense: the loop
        surfaces LESS over time, not more.

        LEVEL GATE SEMANTICS: approving a level means every node
        at depth N is status>=approved and every flag at depth N
        is resolved. The loop will not draft depth N+1 until the
        depth-N gate passes. This is progressive disclosure of
        commitment: no one, human or LLM, evaluates more than one
        delta of abstraction at a time.

        =========================================================
        RELATIONSHIP TO EXISTING CHAPTERS
        =========================================================
        - habitat-workflow: forge is the same discipline with the
          phases driven by code. Session ritual, obituary rule,
          file-don't-fix, and handoff notes are UNCHANGED — forge
          graphs write spec/<pkg> docs on promote, exactly as a
          human session would. The notes docs remain the living
          spec; the forge graph is the WORK-IN-PROGRESS view.
        - ai-authoring: drafters use propose-function / llm-seed /
          agent-session per that chapter's provenance and badge
          rules. forge adds NO new privilege path; machine-authored
          code lands on a branch and promotes through the same
          human gate. The ai badge applies to every forge-drafted
          binding.
        - jobs: forge/run is a job. The runner fiber is the only
          place the loop executes; no new concurrency is added.
        - one-tree: node shapes and placement are specified above
          per one-tree.branching-interaction. Graph state never
          participates in branch lenses (same argument as jobs).
        - lisp-interpreter.effects: forge-effects exposes the
          existing infer; nothing about inference changes.

        =========================================================
        HARD EDGES (non-goals for v1)
        =========================================================
        - NO graph-visualization UI. A tree list with flags is
          enough; the force-directed graph explorer is a later
          nicety, built on evidence of what the queue needs.
        - NO autonomous promote. The loop never calls promote;
          it prepares a branch and a spec doc. The human runs
          pp-sync / promote exactly as today.
        - NO drafter-is-reviewer. The same agent session never
          both writes and judges a node.
        - NO unbounded descent. A graph declares max-depth at
          creation; the loop stalls (status=stalled, judgment
          flag) rather than drafting past it.
        - NO rewriting of borge-the-CLI. .borg files remain the
          git-tracked source of truth for the KERNEL. forge is
          for PRODUCT code (libs/*) where the habitat IS the
          source of truth. The two coexist; forge does not eat
          borge.
   |))
  )

  (subsection graph-model
   (doc "forge-node / forge-flag / forge-graph typed nodes: data.shared placement, denormalized children index, status enum, constraints accumulation")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "image/test: forge node create/update; children index stays consistent with parent links across edits")
    (smoke "pp_eval '(forge/new-graph \"x\")' → row at /data/forge-graph; (forge/add-child …) maintains both directions")))

  (subsection frontier
   (doc "free-vars over parsed bodies; unresolved filter; frontier query; stub-children on unknown refs; cycle detection flags loops")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "image/test: free-vars of (lambda (x) (+ x y)) is {y}; frontier excludes nodes with unresolved deps")
    (test "image/test: drafting a body referencing undefined name 'helper' creates a planned child node named helper")
    (smoke "cycle A→B→A surfaces a cycle flag, not an infinite stub loop")))

  (subsection mechanical-flags
   (doc "capability escalation via forge-effects prim, missing victory, dangling refs, cycles, orphans, size heuristic — each a small fn over the graph; judgment is the human escape hatch")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "image/test: forge-effects prim returns badge for a named binding (pure for +, write-db for create)")
    (test "image/test: child with write-db body under parent declared read-db raises capability flag")
    (smoke "node with empty verify source is victory-flagged before appearing on the approval page")))

  (subsection loop-and-gates
   (doc "forge/step state machine (blocked→flagged→draft→descend); forge/run as a job; tri-state LLM review with separate reviewer session; reroll-with-note constraint accumulation; level-gate semantics")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "image/test: step with open judgment flags is a no-op returning the flag list")
    (test "image/test: reroll appends the note to constraints; next draft prompt contains it")
    (smoke "full descent on a toy graph: root → level 1 approved → stubs → leaf drafted → verified, all via forge/run job")
    (smoke "loop refuses to draft depth N+1 while any depth-N node is unapproved")))

  (subsection human-surface
   (doc "/forge list + /forge/<name> anomaly-first approval page (approve/reroll-with-note/veto per node, approve-all-clean); judgment flags also land in log/issues; NO graph-viz, NO autonomous promote")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (test "image/test: approval page groups nodes by flag type; clean nodes render one-line")
    (smoke "browser: /forge shows a seeded graph; approving a node advances the level gate; reroll dialog accepts a note")
    (smoke "a judgment flag appears in both /forge/<name> and the notes log/issues doc")))

  (subsection non-goals
   (doc "no graph-viz UI v1; no autonomous promote; no drafter-is-reviewer; max-depth hard stop; borge CLI untouched — forge is for product code, .borg stays kernel truth")
   (status planned)
   (verify
    (test "conformance: no forge binding calls promote or merge (surface lint over the package)")
    (test "conformance: reviewer session id ≠ drafter session id on every tri-state review")))

 )
)
